{% extends '@layout/default.html.twig' %}
{% block json_ld %} {{ include('@jsonld/dotkernel/wurfl-cloud-integration-in-dotkernel-1-6-0.jsonld.twig') }} {% endblock %}

{% block title %}Wurfl Cloud Integration in DotKernel 1.6.0{% endblock %}

{% block page_title %}
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="post-hero">
            <a href="{{ url('page::blog') }}" class="breadcrumb-back">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M15 18l-6-6 6-6"/></svg>
                Back to Blog
            </a>
            <div><span class="eyebrow">Dotkernel</span></div>
            <h1>{{ article.title }}</h1>
            <div class="post-meta">
                <span class="text-muted small">
                    By <a href="{{ path('page::author-resource', {slug: article.author.slug}) }}" class="fw-bold link-dark text-decoration-none">{{ article.author.name }}</a>
                    &nbsp;.&nbsp; {{ article.postDate|date('M d, Y') }}
                </span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
    <div class="wrap post-body-wrap">
        <div class="post-layout">

            {{ include('@partial/left-menu.html.twig') }}

            <article class="article">
                <div class="entry">
Another new feature in version 1.6.0 of DotKernel is the integration of Wurfl Cloud.

Wurfl or Wireless Universal Resource FiLe is coming with a new way to deliver their services of device detection and they named it Wurfl Cloud.

Because of the need to detect mobile devices in DotKernel we have integrated Wurfl Cloud as the default detection method.

<!--more-->

If you want to see the Wurfl Cloud library you can find it in the library folder of DotKernel.

Quick steps to have a functional device detection in DotKernel.
<ul>
    <li>register for an Wurfl Cloud account <a title="Scientia Mobile account register" href="https://www.scientiamobile.com/register" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">here</a></li>
    <li>choose <strong>device_os</strong> and <strong>mobile_browser</strong> as your capabilities</li>
    <li>copy your API Key in application.ini in DotKernel</li>
</ul>
<pre>>resources.useragent.wurflcloud.api_key = 000000:XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX</pre>
Now you can test it by changing the user agent in your browser to a mobile device and access your project. Now you can see that you are redirected to the mobile module.

To see how mobile detection is working in DotKernel read this <a title="Detecting Mobile Devices in DotKernel 1.6.0" href="http://www.dotkernel.com/?p=1465">post</a>.
                </div>

                {{ include('@partial/post-nav.html.twig') }}
            </article>

        </div>
    </div>
{% endblock %}
